WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.420
How does the width of the midline between the folds affect whether they can meet?

2
00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:10.020
A simple cartoon shows the idea.

3
00:00:11.033 --> 00:00:15.033
Put your hands flat, thumbs touching, and roll each up onto its thumb edge.

4
00:00:15.833 --> 00:00:18.113
The top edges meet, however broad your hands.

5
00:00:20.383 --> 00:00:22.143
Now put a book between your thumbs.

6
00:00:23.023 --> 00:00:25.323
Upright, the hands stop apart by the book's width.

7
00:00:27.717 --> 00:00:31.157
Broader hands do not help unless you tip them past upright.

8
00:00:32.037 --> 00:00:33.057
The book is the midline.

9
00:00:35.450 --> 00:00:36.850
How does the midline narrow?

10
00:00:37.990 --> 00:00:39.390
Cells crawl in between their side neighbours.

11
00:00:40.350 --> 00:00:41.430
This is intercalation.

12
00:00:42.483 --> 00:00:46.043
Same cells, rearranged: narrower across, longer along.

13
00:00:46.883 --> 00:00:48.203
This is convergent extension.

14
00:00:50.433 --> 00:00:51.693
But which way is across?

15
00:00:52.653 --> 00:00:55.253
On a fly's wing, every hair points toward the tip.

16
00:00:56.113 --> 00:00:57.573
In mutant flies, the hairs swirl.

17
00:01:01.433 --> 00:01:04.713
Frizzled and Dishevelled gather on one side of each cell.

18
00:01:05.713 --> 00:01:07.453
Van Gogh and Prickle gather on the other.

19
00:01:09.733 --> 00:01:13.553
This is planar cell polarity, a sense of direction within a sheet.

20
00:01:15.517 --> 00:01:20.717
In 2002, John Wallingford and Richard Harland asked where in the frog plate it

21
00:01:20.717 --> 00:01:21.717
matters.

22
00:01:22.067 --> 00:01:26.567
Frog cell fates are mapped, so an injection can be aimed at one part of the plate.

23
00:01:28.917 --> 00:01:32.217
They injected a Dishevelled built to block planar polarity.

24
00:01:33.137 --> 00:01:36.317
In some embryos it reached the midline; in others, the sides.

25
00:01:39.067 --> 00:01:44.707
Predict. Which embryos keep an open tube: those blocked at the midline, or those

26
00:01:44.707 --> 00:01:45.707
blocked at the sides?

27
00:01:52.017 --> 00:01:54.437
Blocked at the midline, the plate stayed wide.

28
00:01:55.317 --> 00:01:57.037
The folds rose and moved inward.

29
00:01:57.837 --> 00:01:59.037
But the tube stayed open.

30
00:01:60.000 --> 00:02:04.020
Blocked at the sides, where the folds form and fuse, closure was normal.

31
00:02:08.450 --> 00:02:11.170
The hands rose with the book still between them.

32
00:02:11.790 --> 00:02:13.470
Dishevelled is needed at the midline.

33
00:02:14.983 --> 00:02:16.723
A mouse points the same way.

34
00:02:17.363 --> 00:02:21.663
The loop-tail mouse carries a mutation in Vangl2, a relative of Van Gogh.

35
00:02:23.717 --> 00:02:27.417
With two copies, the tube is open from the midbrain down the spine.

36
00:02:30.150 --> 00:02:34.270
In 2007, Andrew Copp's laboratory labelled that midline.

37
00:02:35.110 --> 00:02:37.850
Before neurulation began, it had failed to extend.

38
00:02:40.050 --> 00:02:41.830
And Closure 1 never happened.

39
00:02:45.233 --> 00:02:47.773
Narrowing starts early and overlaps folding.

40
00:02:48.653 --> 00:02:51.813
The plate must still bend, and the fold tips must stick together.

41
00:02:54.183 --> 00:02:58.283
Takeaway: the midline must be narrow enough for the folds to close the gap.

42
00:02:58.783 --> 00:03:01.583
Frog and mouse both tie closure to midline narrowing.
